#!/bin/sh
# Message of the day of the QR Code & Barcode Scanner 1-Click Droplet.
address=$(hostname -I 2>/dev/null | cut -d' ' -f1)
cat <<MESSAGE

QR Code & Barcode Scanner HTTP service
--------------------------------------
The service answers on port 80 of this Droplet: http://${address:-this-droplet}/
  Health:  curl http://localhost/healthz
  Scan:    curl --data-binary @label.png -H 'content-type: image/png' 'http://localhost/scan?mode=fast'

It has no account or key: anyone who can reach port 80 can scan. The firewall (ufw) allows SSH
and port 80 only. Put TLS and your own access control in front of it before you expose it.

  Status and log:  systemctl status qrscan-http ; journalctl -u qrscan-http
  Settings:        /etc/qrscan-http.env (QRSCAN_HTTP_MAX_* lower the limits), then
                   systemctl restart qrscan-http
  Updates:         apt upgrades the system; a new service version is a new Droplet image.

Documentation and source (AGPL-3.0-or-later):
  https://github.com/qrcode-ai-com/qrcode-barcode-scanner-rust/tree/main/distribution/http

MESSAGE
